<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1732–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibrahim Khalil Khan was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> from 1763, ruling from the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> with the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molla-panah-vagif">Molla Panah Vagif</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. He withstood a siege by Agha Muhammad Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> in 1795. In 1805 he accepted Russian protection by the Treaty of Kurakchay, and he was killed by Russian troops in 1806.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ibrahim Khalil Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javanshir">Javanshir</a> was the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panah-ali-khan">Panah Ali Khan</a> and the second khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> from the Javanshir family. He was among the deportees taken with his father to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Astarabad</a> and returned when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> recognised Panah Ali as khan. His father used his marriages to build alliances: in 1749 he was married to Shahnisa, sister of Nazarali Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahsevan">Shahsevan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, and to Tuti, daughter of Shahverdi Khan of Ganja, and he also married Hurizad, daughter of the Armenian melik Shahnazar II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanda">Varanda</a>. In 1759 he was handed as a hostage to Fath-Ali Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afshar-tribe">Afshar</a> and released by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>With help from Umma Khan of the Avar Khanate he defeated his younger brother Mehr Ali Beg for the khanate. By the 1780s he was one of the most powerful rulers of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, allied with King Heraclius II of Kartli-Kakheti against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan of Quba</a>, until Heraclius accepted Russian protection in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-georgievsk">Treaty of Georgievsk</a> in 1783. From 1775 he also campaigned against the Armenian meliks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>: Melik Yesai of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dizak">Dizak</a> was captured after a long siege and strangled in 1781, and when the meliks secretly wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> in 1783 he seized several of them and the Catholicos Hovhannes, who died in prison.</p><p>In 1795 he refused to submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a>, as the khans of Ganja, Nakhjavan and Erivan did, and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a> through a 31-day siege, later acknowledging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> supremacy in a verbal truce. In 1797 the shah returned and took Shusha, but was assassinated there three days later; Ibrahim, who had fled to the Avar Khanate, came back, gave him an honourable burial, and gave a daughter in marriage to his successor, Fath Ali Shah Qajar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War</a>, General Tsitsianov promised to recognise him as khan, and after their agreement Russian troops garrisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>. After Tsitsianov's death in 1806 Ibrahim renounced his allegiance and asked the shah for help, camping outside the fortress as a Persian army approached. On 12 June 1806 Russian troops under Dmitry Lisanevich attacked the camp and killed him with one of his wives, a daughter and his youngest son. The Russians made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehdi-qoli-khan">Mehdi Qoli Khan Javanshir</a> khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ibrahim Khalil Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ibrahim Khalil Khan</b>, from 1763, was a powerful ruler, and his vizier, the poet Molla Panah Vagif, shaped court culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/karabakh-khanate">Karabakh Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.76,46.75&amp;year=1780" aria-label="Shusha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5703 740 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ibrahim Khalil Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.76,46.75&amp;year=1780">What was here in 1780? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
